(* ============================================================================
   PART 0: LAYOUT LAYER (L0) -- LINE AUTOMATON (NORMATIVE)
   ============================================================================

   The block productions (PARTS 1-2) are context-free only OVER THE OUTPUT OF
   THIS LAYER. Indentation, container prefixes, and lazy continuation are 2D
   properties no string grammar can express (they need column arithmetic and
   carried container state); L0 states them as a deterministic one-pass line
   automaton instead of prose.

   INPUT.  Lines, split on `newline` as that production defines it below.

           A LEADING BYTE ORDER MARK IS STRIPPED -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-001]. A single U+FEFF
           at the very start of the document is removed before the first line
           is read, so `<BOM># T` is a heading and not paragraph text. ONE, and
           only there: a U+FEFF anywhere else is an ordinary zero-width
           character, and zero-width characters (U+200B, U+FEFF) are NOT
           whitespace and ARE ordinary destination characters.

           A NULL IS REPLACED BEFORE THE FIRST LINE IS READ -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-002].
           Every U+0000 in the source becomes U+FFFD REPLACEMENT CHARACTER,
           wherever it stands, before the first line is read. It is therefore
           not content, and it is the ONE C0 control PART 9 §29 does not make
           content; CARVE-P9-061 carries that carve-out. EVERY occurrence is replaced,
           one U+0000 to one U+FFFD, so the transform does not change source
           offsets.

           ILL-FORMED INPUT IS REPLACED BEFORE THE FIRST LINE IS READ -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-023].
           A document is a sequence of Unicode scalar values. Every ill-formed
           sequence in the input becomes U+FFFD REPLACEMENT CHARACTER before
           the first line is read, the same way U+0000 is replaced under
           CARVE-P0-002. Two shapes are ill-formed: an invalid UTF-8 byte
           sequence in a file or stream, which takes one U+FFFD per maximal
           subpart (the count the WHATWG UTF-8 decoder gives), and a lone
           surrogate in a string handed to an API, which takes one U+FFFD per
           unpaired code unit. Ill-formed input is replaced, never rejected,
           so every HTML and AST string a processor emits is well-formed. A
           paired surrogate is a well-formed astral character and is left
           alone, and a noncharacter (U+FFFE, U+FDD0) is a scalar value and
           therefore content. The rule covers the document handed to the
           processor, not an included file: an include whose bytes are not
           well-formed UTF-8 is refused as non-text under PART 6 I7, not
           replaced.

           Column arithmetic is PART 9 §24 C1. A TAB IS SYNTAX ONLY IN THE
           LEADING RUN (CARVE-P7-001).
   STATE.  open := stack of open containers, bottom-up (document ->
           innermost), plus the current leaf (none | paragraph | heading |
           ...). Container kinds and the per-line prefix each demands:
             block quote    marker `>` [end of line | one space]
             list item      indentation to content_column (PART 9 §24 C3)
             definition     indentation to the body column its separator
               body         sets (CARVE-P2-016)
             footnote def   marker-body content column two past its authored
                            marker column; exactly two columns consumed
                            (PART 9 §24 C3)
             opaque body    (code / raw / matched comment span) -- no
                            per-line prefix; verbatim up to its where-guarded
                            closer
             colon body     (admonition / div / figure group / fenced quote /
                            hard-break block / line block) -- no per-line
                            prefix; parsed as blocks (a line block as lines,
                            PART 9 §23) up to its exact-length closer or the
                            end of its host.

           A list item, a definition body and a footnote body are the
           INDENTATION CONTAINERS.

           EVERY FRAME CARRIES OWNERSHIP AND PARAGRAPH STATE SEPARATELY --
           NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-003]. A frame is the record

             owner              document | quote | list_item |
                                definition_body | footnote_body |
                                opaque_body | colon_body
             base_column        authored column at which this frame opened
             content_column     minimum column of ordinary child blocks
             container_open     whether the frame may own another line
             paragraph_open     whether its current leaf accepts lazy text
             last_boundary_kind ordinary | blank | line_comment |
                                fenced_comment | definition | attribute_line |
                                heading | thematic_break | quote | code_fence |
                                raw_fence | colon_fence | table | nested_list |
                                continuation | end

           `container_open` is inferred from `paragraph_open` only below the
           content column (S4). Elsewhere `paragraph_open` describes an
           operation available inside a frame that has already survived; it
           does not keep that frame alive.

           T  TRANSITIONS. Once a line or block has its owner, the boundary it
           forms sets both facts in every frame that contains it. `C` means
           the frame stays open after the boundary; `P` independently says
           whether a paragraph is open. `deepest P` means the last child
           container is followed to its deepest leaf. The table applies to
           list items, quotes, definition bodies and footnote bodies alike.

             preceding boundary       container_open   paragraph_open
             ordinary line             C                yes
             blank line                C                no
             line comment              C                no
             fenced comment            C                no
             invisible definition      C                no, for full extent
             attribute line            C                no
             heading                   C                no
             thematic break            C                no
             block quote               C                deepest P
             closed code/raw fence     C                no
             closed colon fence        C                no
             table                     C                no
             nested list               C                deepest P
             continuation marker       C                deepest attached P
             end of input              no               no

           A closed block therefore has ONE effect in every containing frame:
           it leaves the frame open and leaves no paragraph open. A following
           line may close the frame because its column is owned by an ancestor;
           the closed block itself never closes its owner.

   BLOCK STRUCTURE IS ONE ORDERED PROCEDURE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-024].
   One rule decides ownership for every container (markup-carve/carve#3885):

     A container's content column is fixed by the line that opens it. A
     later line belongs to the deepest open container whose content column
     it reaches. Otherwise it continues an open paragraph lazily, or it
     closes containers.

   No family, depth or marker adds a second way to belong, and nothing set
   after the opening line moves a content column. For each normalized line,
   execute S1 to S5 once, in order and without backtracking, and apply T at
   each boundary. Only a construct's declared closer guard may read ahead.
   Content columns:

     document          0
     block quote       after `>` and one optional space; a quote is reached
                       by its marker, never by indentation
     list item         after the marker and its separator (PART 9 §24 C3)
     definition body   after `:` and its separator (CARVE-P2-016)
     footnote body     two past the column of `[` (CARVE-P9-083)
     colon body        the content column of the owner its opener was read
                       in; S3 moves its lines left

     S1 PAYLOAD. If a code, raw or comment fence is open, test the line
        against its closer first. A nonblank line that misses the fence's
        owner in S2 ends the fence; nothing else ends a code or raw fence
        without its closer, so an unclosed one runs to the end of its
        container and a host's colon closer inside it is payload. Any other payload
        line opens nothing and changes no frame state. A colon body is not a
        payload: its lines go on to S2.

        COMMENTS ARE CLASSIFIED BEFORE BLOCK OWNERSHIP -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-005].
        Honor an active payload first. Then recognize `%%` and a matched
        `%%%` span as comment tokens before S3 classifies the visible
        residue. Record the comment node, its authored spelling and its
        complete source extent, and remove it from visible classification
        and rendering: this is erasure from layout, never from the AST or the
        source map. A matched `%%%` span carries its opener, opaque body and
        exact-width closer as one extent across authored indentation. An
        opener without an exact-width closer is one `%%` line comment.
        Comment delimiters inside code, raw content or another payload are
        literal bytes.

        A comment is still an ordinary line for ownership. S2 selects its
        owner, it takes the `line_comment` / `fenced_comment` row of T there,
        and below a container's content column it does not fold and closes
        that container (S5). A comment between two sibling items closes the
        first one and leaves the list open for the second.

     S2 OWNER. Walk the open frames from the outside in. A quote frame
        consumes its marker; any other frame consumes indentation up to its
        content column (PART 9 §24 C5). Stop at the first frame the line does
        not reach. A lone `+` at the marker column of an open list item,
        quote, definition body or footnote body reaches that frame when PART 9
        §17 L3 attaches its next line; any other `+` is a letter. The owner is the deepest frame reached. A blank line reaches
        every indentation frame and no unmarked quote: without its `>` it
        ends a quote and the containers opened inside it.

        A NEW MARKER DOES NOT REACH A DEAD CONTAINER'S COLUMN -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-019].
        A blank ends a block quote and the containers opened inside it. A
        later quote marker opens a new quote without inheriting their
        columns. In

            > - x

            >   [r]: /u

            > [r][]

        no item is open when the definition is read, so it is read in the
        second quote, past that quote's content column, and registers there
        (S3).

        AT OR PAST MEANS THE DEEPEST COLUMN THE LINE REACHES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-020].
        A line is read in the innermost open container whose content column
        it reaches. A deeper container the line does not reach neither owns
        nor withholds it. In `- - x` / `   [r]: /url` the items' content
        columns are 2 and 4, and the definition at column 3 registers in the
        outer item. Past a content column, extra indentation may change the
        owner but never takes the line out of every owner.

     S3 FORM. Classify the rest of the line in the owner's coordinates:
        blank, comment, block attribute, reference or footnote definition,
        closer, opener, caption, `+`, or ordinary text.

        Leading space before an opener does not stop it from opening, at
        every level, the document and quotes included: an opener past the
        owner's content column opens in the owner and is measured from its
        own line. Every line the new block owns moves left by the space that
        preceded the opener, so the block reads as if written at the owner's
        content column. A line with less indentation loses only what it has,
        which is how a code fence strips its own indentation:

            - x

                 ```
                 code
               less
                   more
                 ```

        The code is `code`, `less`, `  more`. `  # h` at document level is a
        heading, and a `> q` indented under a `:: t` term opens a quote in
        the enclosing container. Under an open paragraph a list marker is text
        unless the owner is a list item, and a valid code or raw opener
        interrupts it (PART 9 §10 I4). The list item is the only exception
        (the tight sublist of PART 9 §24 C3), and it covers only the item's
        own open paragraph. A description body, a quote and a footnote body
        take none, and an open paragraph of a note inside an item is not the
        item's own: in `:: t` / `:  [^q]: note` / `   * child` the line
        reaches the description, misses the note, and `* child` is text of
        the note's open paragraph, and `- [^q]: note` / `  * child` folds the
        same way. A list marker at a
        list item's content column opens a child list; a sibling marker at the
        item's marker column continues the enclosing list, and siblings group
        by the container they open in, not by the column they are written at.
        A `: ` description is such a sibling too: where the owner S2 selects
        holds an open term or description, a `+`-filled one included
        (carve#4408), a `: ` line opens the next description of that list at
        any column and is not a lazy line, so `:: t` / `: a` / ` : b` and
        `:: t` / `: +` / `# H` / ` : b` each give two descriptions. A line at or past an
        open description's body column is read inside that body (`:: t` /
        `: a` / `  : b`), and one whose owner holds no open term or
        description folds lazily (`- item` / `  :: t` / `  : a` / ` : b`).
        At a surviving container's marker column the line ends the item
        instead, as a list marker there does, and with no open term in the
        surviving context it is paragraph text: `- item` / `  :: t` /
        `  : a` / `: b` renders `: b` as a paragraph after the list (corpus
        657). Under an open quote paragraph it folds at every column (S4).

        AT A CONTAINER'S CONTENT COLUMN, A BLOCK ENDS THE PARAGRAPH IT SITS
        UNDER -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-011]. A block read in a container, at or
        past its content column, ends the preceding paragraph and leaves the
        container open, whether the block is visible or invisible. Comments,
        link and footnote definitions and attribute blocks all apply. The
        transition covers the block's entire extent (CARVE-P0-012).

        A DEFINITION AT THE CONTENT COLUMN ENDS THE PARAGRAPH, NOT THE
        CONTAINER -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-010]. A link or footnote definition
        registers in the owner S2 selects and only there. `- a` / `  [r]: /u`
        / `tail` registers `r`, then ends the item and publishes `tail` at
        document level. Below every open content column a definition
        interrupts (S4), so `- a` / ` [r]: /u` / `tail` closes the item and
        registers `r` in the document. Abbreviation definitions are recognized
        only at document level (CARVE-P12-026), which is where a line that misses
        every frame stands: `- a` / `*[A]: b` and `- a` / ` *[A]: b` close the item
        and define `A`, while `- a` / `  *[A]: b` stays item text. A definition either registers
        or remains visible text; it never disappears from both.

        A FLOATING ATTRIBUTE BLOCK DOES NOT CHANGE THE CONTENT COLUMN --
        NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-008]. Marker-attached attributes contribute zero
        width to the content column, and a task checkbox is content, not
        marker width. `-{#item} {#next}` / `  # H` keeps the item open and
        attaches `{#next}` to the heading. One column further left the
        heading misses the item: the item closes, its pending attribute is
        discarded (CARVE-P9-024), and the heading opens in the document.

     S4 LAZY. If the line missed a frame below its owner, it folds into the
        deepest open paragraph when that paragraph is open, the line is not
        blank, and its form does not interrupt a paragraph. The deepest
        paragraph is found by following the last child container to its leaf
        at every depth. The test is the same at every depth and in every
        family. A list marker interrupts only as the sibling of an open list.
        Headings, thematic breaks, quotes, terms, tables, valid colon openers,
        code and raw openers, comments, attributes and
        definitions interrupt. An invalid opener is text. A fold closes
        nothing and changes the leaf operation, never which container
        survives.

        NO STORED CONTINUATION CLAIM, NO LAZY LINE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-006].
        A lazy line needs an open paragraph at the deepest leaf; a surviving
        frame does not by itself admit one, and S4 never searches earlier
        blocks or ancestor paragraphs. A boundary whose T row closes the
        paragraph leaves none, and an empty or closed container supplies none
        (CARVE-P0-017). For `. >` / `X` the empty quote leaves nothing open,
        so `X` starts a document paragraph; `- > q` / `lazy` folds into the
        quoted paragraph. An ended descendant supplies no paragraph to an
        enclosing item, but a later line at that item's content column starts
        one: `- - - ~~~` / `  tail` / `tail` puts both `tail` lines in the
        outer item.

        A LAZY FOLD KEEPS THE CONTAINER OPEN FOR FOLLOWING LINES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-015].
        A lazy fold closes no container, so a later line at the container's
        content column is still inside it:

            - x
              :::
              a
            d
              b
              :::

        `a`, `d` and `b` form one paragraph in one div. With no open
        paragraph, the flush-left line ends the container and later lines are
        outside it.

        A HEADING IS THE VISIBLE CONTROL -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-012]. A
        heading leaves no paragraph open, so a following line that misses its
        container closes it rather than folding. This is the lazy test of S4
        applied to a closed leaf, in a footnote body as in every other
        container; it is not a family exception.

     S5 APPLY. Otherwise close every missed frame and apply the form in the
        owner. A blank or invisible line ends the open paragraph. A container
        opener pushes a frame and classifies the rest of its marker line in
        that frame. A leaf opener ends the open leaf and starts a new one.
        Ordinary text starts or extends a paragraph. A closed block leaves no
        open paragraph. End of input closes every frame.

        THE MARKER LINE'S CONTENT IS THE ITEM'S FIRST BLOCK -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-007].
        Marker-line content is the container's first block and answers S4
        like any other block. Headings, tables, thematic breaks, comments,
        definitions and floating attributes leave no paragraph open, so a
        following line below the content column closes the container.
        Ordinary prose, or a container ending in an open paragraph, permits a
        lazy fold. Definitions are collected where written (CARVE-P9-034).

        A FENCED BODY IS NOT A PARAGRAPH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-013]. A
        payload holds no open paragraph for S4 to continue. In

            - ```
            x
            ```

        `x` misses the item, so the fence and the item end with an empty code
        block; `x` is document text, and the trailing run opens an empty code
        block (PART 9 §10 I4).

        FENCE KIND DOES NOT DETERMINE CONTAINER REACH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-014].
        Code and colon bodies answer CARVE-P0-013's test alike. A code fence
        interrupts an open paragraph whether or not a closer follows (PART 9
        §10 I4), and a line that misses its owner ends it. In

            - a
              ```
              b
             y
              ```

        `b` is a code block, ` y` ends it and the item, and the last run
        opens an unclosed code block after the document paragraph `y`.

        CONTAINER KIND DOES NOT DETERMINE CONTAINER REACH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-017].
        Quotes answer CARVE-P0-013's test too. `> quote` / `> ::: note` /
        `tail` ends the quote, as does `> body` / `> :::` before `tail`. An
        empty or closed colon container leaves no open paragraph.

   FAMILIES. Every family follows S1 to S5. Each entry gives only what the
   family adds. The bracketed ids name its cells: `start` is what opens the
   block, `keep` what continues it, `end` what ends it. A cell with no text of
   its own follows S2 and S4 (keep) or S5 and end of input (end). The step ids
   S1 to S5 and T, and these cell ids, are stable: a decision trace
   (docs/decision-trace.md) cites them, so they are never renumbered.

     list item        [B-item-start] Starts with a valid bullet, task,
                      decimal, alphabetic, roman or bare-dot marker with
                      content; a marker with no content is text. [B-list-keep]
                      Items in one container group when marker kind,
                      delimiter, dialect and task state agree. A blank
                      between items or before a second paragraph makes the
                      list loose; a blank before a child block alone does
                      not. [B-list-end] Three consecutive blanks before a
                      sibling start a new list (PART 9 §11, §17).
     term             [B-term-start] `:: ` with content; a leaf with inline
                      content. [B-term-keep] It continues by the paragraph
                      test and has no ownership of its own, but a lazy line
                      continues it only as plain text: a list marker that
                      misses the term's quote or footnote body ends that
                      container and reads as it would without it, and so
                      does a `: ` separator that misses the term's quote;
                      [B-term-end] it
                      ends at anything that ends a paragraph or at a `: ` body
                      marker in the same owner.
     definition body  [B-dd-start] `: ` with content, directly after a term
                      or body in the same owner. [B-dd-end] It ends at the
                      next term or body marker in that owner, a missed line
                      that does not fold, the host's end or end of input.
     footnote body    [B-footnote-start] `[^label]: `. [B-footnote-keep] Lazy
                      lines follow S4 as in every other container. A
                      referenced body renders as an endnote.
     block quote      [B-quote-start] `>` then a space or line end; each `>`
                      opens one level. [B-quote-end] An unmarked blank ends
                      the quote; [B-quote-keep] a marked blank keeps it and
                      ends its paragraph.
     colon fence      [B-div-start] A valid run of three or more colons.
                      [B-div-end] It ends at an exact-length colon run read in
                      the same owner, the host's end or end of input.
                      [B-div-keep] `|` mode keeps its lines as payload and,
                      like the other modes, which read blocks, takes a lazy
                      line while its stanza is open (PART 2 `line_block`).
     code, raw and    [B-code-start] [B-comment-start] A valid code or raw
       comment fence  opener; a comment opener with a matching closer.
                      [B-code-keep] [B-comment-keep] The payload is opaque and
                      takes no lazy line. [B-code-end] [B-comment-end] It ends
                      at its closer or by S1.
     table            [B-table-start] A valid pipe row, or a selected CSV or
                      TSV fence. [B-table-end] It ends at the first line that
                      is not a row; [B-table-keep] a continuation row needs a
                      body row above it.
     caption          [B-caption-start] `^ ` with content after an eligible
                      host. One blank between host and marker is allowed, two
                      detach.
     `+`              [B-plus-start] A lone `+` at the marker column of an
                      open list item, quote, definition body or
                      footnote body, or a `+` that is the whole content of
                      such a marker line (`- +`, `> +`, `: +`, `[^a]: +`),
                      offers the container its next block (PART 9 §17,
                      CARVE-P9-067). A refused `+` is a plain letter
                      (carve#4396).

   OUTPUT. The layout-resolved line stream (equivalently: INDENT / DEDENT /
           CONT events plus line residues). PARTS 1-2 parse blocks over it;
           inline parsing (PART 3) runs per finished block -- the two-phase
           model of PART 8.
   ============================================================================ *)
